    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <desc>A lab someone sent you, whose disk image isn't in the
    archive.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Import a lab without its image</title>
  <p>A colleague sends you <file>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp</file>, a lab of
  four virtual machines, <sys>node1</sys> to <sys>node4</sys>, five
  switches, a switch wrapper and three channel emulators. The machines run on private copies of one disk image,
  <sys>debian13</sys>, a file of a few gigabytes that the archive doesn't
  hold: the private copies are in it, with what was done in each machine,
  but not the image they are made on. Your colleague sends the image apart,
  or you have it already.</p>
  <p>The lab was made with Virtualbricks 2.1, and Virtualbricks converts it
  while it reads the archive. An archive of this version imports the same
  way.</p>
  <section id="open">
    <title>Open the archive</title>
    <steps>
      <item><p>Choose <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Import…</gui></guiseq>,
      or <gui>Import…</gui> in the Projects window. The Import Project window
      asks for the archive.</p></item>
      <item><p>Click the button of the file, which says
      <gui>(None)</gui>, and choose
      <file>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp</file>. The window reads the archive,
      then shows what it holds.</p></item>
    </steps>
    <figure>
      <title>The Import Project window, with the lab</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import.png">
        <p>The Import Project window for ~/DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp: the
        name DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026; 3 netemu, 4 qemu, 5 switch and 1
        switchwrapper; the image debian13, used by node1.hda to node4.hda,
        with Copy grey and Leave unset chosen; Open the project after the
        import, on.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section id="options">
    <title>What the window offers</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Name</gui></title>
        <p><sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026</sys>, the name of the file. If a
        project has it already, the window suggests
        <sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026-2</sys>: an import never replaces a
        project. You can change it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The bricks</title>
        <p>Below the name, how many bricks of each kind the project has, by
        the names of the project file: <sys>netemu</sys> are the channel
        emulators, <sys>qemu</sys> the virtual machines.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Images</gui></title>
        <p>A row for <sys>debian13</sys>, used by the disk
        <sys>hda</sys> of each of the four machines, with three
        choices:</p>
        <list>
          <item><p><gui>Copy</gui>, from the archive to the shared images of
          your workspace;</p></item>
          <item><p><gui>Use a file…</gui> of this computer;</p></item>
          <item><p><gui>Leave unset</gui>, for its disks to get an image
          later.</p></item>
        </list>
        <p><gui>Leave unset</gui> is chosen: the window looked for the image
        where the project had it,
        <file>/home/carlo/VB_images/debian13.qcow2</file> on your colleague's
        computer, and in your shared images, under the name
        <file>debian13.qcow2</file>, and found it in neither. Had it
        found it, <gui>Use a file…</gui> would be chosen, with that
        file.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Open the project after the import</gui></title>
        <p>On: once imported, the lab opens in the main window.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="greyed">
    <title>What is grey, and why</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Copy</gui></title>
        <p>The archive doesn't hold the image: there is nothing to copy. Its
        tooltip says <gui>Not in the archive</gui>. In a large archive, the
        window may look for the image for a while: <gui>Copy</gui> is chosen
        meanwhile, the row says <gui>Looking in the archive…</gui>, and
        <gui>Copy</gui> turns grey once the window has read the archive to
        its end.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Import</gui></title>
        <p>Grey until the window has read the archive, and whenever something
        stops the import; the window says what. Under the name: a name that
        is empty, has a <sys>/</sys>, starts with a dot, is too long, or is a
        project's already. At the bottom, in red: a file chosen with <gui>Use a
        file…</gui> that isn't there any more, or a name too long for the
        names of the bricks. Their sockets are in a folder named after the
        project, and the path of a socket has at most 107 bytes; the longest
        name of this lab, <sys>sat_channel_emulator</sys>, has 20.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>This computer's paths</gui></title>
        <p>Not there at all for this lab. It shows when the project names the
        folders of QEMU and VDE of the computer it comes from, and they
        aren't yours; a project of Virtualbricks 2.1 doesn't name them.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Import…</gui></guiseq></title>
        <p>Grey in the windows of another Virtualbricks, opened with
        <cmd>--connect</cmd>: the archives stay on the computer they are
        on, for now. See <link xref="remote"/>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="image">
    <title>Choose the image</title>
    <p>This is the choice that matters.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>You have the image</title>
        <p>Click <gui>Use a file…</gui> and choose it; the row then says
        <gui>Uses</gui> and its path. The import points the private copies
        of the four machines at it, so that they keep what was done in them.
        It must be the image the lab was made on, a copy of your colleague's
        file: a private copy holds only the changes over its image, and over
        another one the machine finds a damaged disk.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>You don't have it</title>
        <p>Keep <gui>Leave unset</gui>. The project comes without the image
        <sys>debian13</sys>, and the disks of the four machines without an
        image. Their private copies come as the archive has them, made on the
        file of your colleague's computer. When you give the disks an image,
        in their settings, the first start of each machine moves its private
        copy aside, as <file>node1_hda.cow.bak-</file> and the date, and
        makes a new one, empty: the machine starts from the image as it is,
        without what was done in it.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <note style="tip">
      <p>If you get the image later, import the archive again then, with
      <gui>Use a file…</gui>, and remove the first import.</p>
    </note>
  </section>
  <section id="import">
    <title>Import</title>
    <steps>
      <item><p>Click <gui>Import</gui>. The window shows the progress, and
      <gui>Cancel</gui> becomes <gui>Stop</gui>: an import stopped removes
      what it wrote. You can close the window, and let the import
      finish.</p></item>
      <item><p>The main window shows <sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026</sys>, with
      its thirteen bricks, all stopped.</p></item>
      <item><p>Open <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Logs</gui></guiseq>: the
      import says what needs your attention. With the image left unset:
      <sys>left unset: give its disks an image in their settings</sys>, and
      for each machine <sys>its image is unset, the private disk is left as
      it is</sys>.</p></item>
    </steps>
    <p>If a brick of the project open before runs, the lab is imported but
    doesn't open: stop the bricks, then open it from the Projects window.</p>
    <p>In the <gui>Bricks</gui> tab, the start button of
    <sys>switchwrapper</sys> is grey: a switch wrapper is a switch that
    another program runs, at <file>/var/run/switch/sck</file>, and it runs
    while that program's switch is there. See <link xref="kinds"/>.</p>
  </section>
</page>
